c(21,'01','Explain the error in calling a tile manufacturer’s acquisition of its clay supplier forward integration.','It is backward vertical integration because the acquired activity supplies inputs at an earlier stage. Forward integration would move from manufacturing towards customers, such as acquiring a retailer.',['Confusion (1): supplier direction mistaken for forward. Boundary/test (1): earlier input stage versus later retail stage.'])
for n,l,points,term in [(8,'02','100/N$12, 200/N$9, 300/N$8','economies'),(10,'03','300/N$8, 400/N$9, 500/N$11','diseconomies')]:
 ans=('Plot falling long-run average cost across the three points. Suitable technical specialisation or purchasing gains at larger scale can lower cost per unit; the schedule alone does not isolate the cause.' if l=='02' else 'Plot rising long-run average cost across the three points. Communication delays and coordination errors can add cost per unit as the organisation expands; this differs from adding workers to fixed equipment.')
 prompt='Explain '+term+' of scale using an original construction: plot output horizontally and long-run average cost in N$ per unit vertically for these invented output/cost points: '+points+'. All inputs can be adjusted. Describe the pattern and an economic mechanism.'
 if n==10:prompt='Explain an invented large organisation’s diseconomies using a learner-constructed cost illustration. Its adjustable-input long-run alternatives are 300 units costing N$8 each, 400 costing N$9 each and 500 costing N$11 each. Construct labelled output/cost axes, mark these positions and interpret coordination delays as a possible cause of the rising section.'
 c(n,l,prompt,ans,['Axes/labels (2): output horizontally (1), long-run average cost N$/unit vertically (1). Initial position/original curve (1): first stated point. Change (2): remaining points correctly placed (1), falling/rising pattern identified (1). Explanation/interpretation (2): appropriate scale mechanism (1), long-run input-adjustment condition rather than fixed capacity (1). Total 7.'])
c(9,'02','Analyse an invented long-run expansion from 100 units at N$12 average cost to 300 at N$8, where specialist equipment lowers unit cost and all inputs can adjust.','Average cost falls N$4 per unit, illustrating technical economies of scale under the stated conditions. Total cost nevertheless rises N$1,200 to N$2,400, so the benefit is per unit rather than a smaller total bill.',['Pattern (1): average cost falls. Evidence (1): N$12 to N$8. Explanation (2): technical scale gain (1), total cost can rise with volume (1).'])
c(11,'03','Analyse a long-run expansion where output rises from 300 to 500 units and average cost from N$8 to N$11, with documented coordination delays and all inputs adjustable.','Unit cost rises N$3, consistent with diseconomies of scale. Coordination delays add resources and rework per unit; the adjustable-input long-run case is different from diminishing returns to labour with fixed equipment.',['Pattern (1): higher long-run unit cost. Evidence (1): N$8 to N$11. Explanation (2): coordination delays (1), distinct from fixed-input diminishing returns (1).'])
c(22,'03','Explain the confusion in calling a rise in long-run average cost as scale increases an economy of scale.','A rise is a diseconomy of scale. An economy lowers long-run average cost; the direction of the unit-cost change distinguishes them.',['Confusion (1): economies label applied to rising cost. Boundary/test (1): rising versus falling long-run average cost.'])
c(12,'04','Explain the net average-cost effect when expansion creates N$3 per unit technical/purchasing savings and N$1 extra coordination cost per unit, with other relevant costs unchanged.','The N$1 diseconomy offsets part of the N$3 saving, leaving a net N$2 reduction in long-run average cost. The balance, rather than the existence of either effect alone, determines the direction.',['Starting change (1): expansion brings both effects. Mechanism (2): N$3 saving (1), N$1 offset (1). Consequence (1): N$2 net unit-cost fall.'])
c(13,'04','Explain why a fall in average cost from N$12 at 100 units to N$8 at 300 does not mean a lower total cost.','Despite economies of scale lowering the unit figure, total cost is average cost multiplied by output: N$1,200 initially and N$2,400 afterwards. Larger output can more than offset the lower per-unit amount.',['Context (1): multiply by corresponding output. Reason/conclusion (1): total doubles despite falling unit cost.'])
c(14,'05','Identify two distinct constraints on firm size, each with a brief example.','A narrow market can leave large capacity without enough buyers. Limited finance can prevent purchase of the equipment required for expansion.',['Feature/example (1): market size and demand constraint. Distinct characteristic/example (1): capital availability and equipment constraint.'])
pairdata=[(15,16,'05-M01','strong sustained orders','Confirmed saleable demand supports utilisation of larger capacity, making scale gains more feasible. A narrow or temporary market could leave that capacity idle.','Explain why a customised tile firm with only a few stable monthly orders may remain small despite access to a larger machine.','A larger machine does not create buyers. Limited saleable demand can leave it underused and make expansion unattractive.'),(17,18,'05-M02','N$200,000 expansion cost but only N$60,000 available and no other funding','The N$140,000 funding gap prevents purchase of the proposed capacity, so market demand alone cannot make expansion feasible. Additional affordable funding or a smaller stage would be required.','Explain the financing constraint where equipment requires N$200,000 but only N$60,000 is available and no additional funding is offered.','The N$140,000 gap prevents this acquisition; expected demand does not itself supply the funds.'),(19,20,'05-M03','several outlets without shared stock records, causing duplicated orders and delays','Expansion increases coordination demands. Missing shared records create errors and rework, adding cost per unit and limiting manageable size until organisation improves.','Explain how clear responsibilities and a suitable shared stock record could ease growth problems caused by duplicated orders.','They align decisions and information across outlets, reducing duplication and delay so the organisation can handle larger operations more effectively.')]
